  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Read the following statements:<br />(A) Volume of the nucleus is directly proportional to the mass number.<br />(B) Volume of the nucleus is independent of mass number.<br />(C) Density of the nucleus is directly proportional to the mass number.<br />(D) Density of the nucleus is directly proportional to the cube root of the mass number.<br />(E) Density of the nucleus is independent of the mass number.</p><p>Choose the correct option from the following options.</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "(A) and (D) only.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(A) and (E) only.",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "(B) and (E) only.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(A) and (C) only",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Radius of nucleus is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup></math>, where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;mass number of nucleus.</p><p>Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup></math></p><p>Volume of nucleus is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.</p><p>Now, the density of nucleus is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>&#960;</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>&#960;</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;density is independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>.</p><p>Hence, only&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>A</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>E</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;are correct.</p></blockquote>"
  }
